  "answer": "The test imports `sumEntries` from `src/index`, so I'll rename the helper in `src/core.ts`, update `src/index.ts` to use and re-export it, keep `total` as a wrapper, and leave the test untouched.\nBoth files updated. Let me verify no `computeTotal` references remain and run the test.\nThe glob filter may not have matched recursively — retrying without it.\nNo `computeTotal` remains, and the test file is unchanged. Running the test:\nDone — the rename is complete and the test passes.\n\n## What changed\n\n**`src/core.ts`**\n- Renamed the exported helper `computeTotal` → `sumEntries`. The implementation (reduce over values with a `0` seed) is untouched.\n\n**`src/index.ts`**\n- Import updated to `import { sumEntries } from './core'`.\n- Added `export { sumEntries }` so the helper is exported under the new name from the package entry point — this is what makes the existing test import resolve.\n- The `total` wrapper keeps its exact behavior: `export const total = (values: number[]) => sumEntries(values)` (same arrow-function shape, just calling the renamed helper).\n\n**`test/oracle.test.ts`** — unchanged (byte-identical, same SHA-256 as before), still importing `total` and `sumEntries` from `../src/index`.\n\n## Verification\n- A workspace-wide search confirms `computeTotal` no longer appears anywhere; `sumEntries` is defined in `core.ts`, re-exported via `index.ts`, and used by the test.\n- `bun test` result: **1 pass, 0 fail, 2 expect() calls** (`(pass) sum entries [0.19ms]`), covering both the direct `sumEntries([1,2,3]) === 6` call and the `total([4,5]) === 9` wrapper."
